   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law and the insurance law, in relation to\nutilization review of coverage of nursing home care following an inpatient\nhospital admission",
    "basePrintNo": "S1810",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1810-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4903, Pub Health L; amd §4903, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1810\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law and the insurance law, in relation\nto utilization review of coverage of nursing home care following an\ninpatient hospital admission\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo decrease delays for post-acute care by adding nursing home care to\nthe types of care for which an insurer must make a determination on\nauthorization within twenty-four hours.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends paragraphs (a) and (c) of subdivision 3 of section\n4903 of the Public Health Law by requiring that health care plans make a\ndetermination involving continued or extended post acute nursing home\ncare within twenty four hours of receipt of request for services and by\nprohibiting denial of nursing home coverage on the basis of medical\nnecessity or lack of prior authorization while the determination is\npending.\n\nSection two makes the aforementioned amendments to paragraphs 1 and 3 of\nsubsection (c) of section 4903 of the Insurance Law.\n\nSection three provides that this act shall take effect 90 days after\nenactment.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMany patients require home care or skilled nursing home care upon\ndischarge from the hospital. Hospitals increasingly experience delays in\nobtaining authorizations from insurers for post-acute care. Since most\ninsurers pay hospitals based on diagnosis-related group systems, it does\nnot cost the insurer any more to have patients stay in the hospital for\na few extra days and doing so can reduce insurer costs for post-acute\ncare. This is not good for patients, nor is it good for hospitals trying\nto operate as efficiently as possible.\n\nState law has been modified twice in recent years to tighten timeframes\nfor authorization for post-Acute home care and substance abuse services.\nThis legislation will extend the tightened timeframe to skilled nursing\nhome services, requiring insurers to make determinations on requests for\nnursing home care following an inpatient admission within 24 hours of\nreceipt of a request as long as the request is made at least 24 hours\nprior to discharge from the hospital. This change is particularly impor-\ntant now that the Medicaid nursing home benefit has moved from Medicaid\nfee-for-service to managed care.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.6492 of 2016\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n90 days after enactment\n",
